{
  "version": 1,
  "note": "HL10 section 7.5. The hidden prerequisite of every language textbook: it assumes the reader already knows grammar VOCABULARY. \"The first-person singular present indicative of a regular -ar verb\" spends six technical terms on one form, and a beginner who never studied grammar understands none of them. So the metalanguage is itself a taught ramp -- one new term per lesson, each introduced only AFTER the learner can already do the thing it names, and re-glossed on reappearance after a long gap. `plainAlternative` is what a lesson must say INSTEAD, before the term is introduced. It is the field that makes the rule actionable rather than merely restrictive.",
  "measuredAt": "2026-08-10",
  "corpusFinding": "Measured before authoring: `infinitive` appears in 87 lessons, `participle` 67, `dative` 53, `conjugation` 21, `accusative` 19, `declension` 14, `imperfect` 14, `genitive` 13, `nominative` 12 -- with nothing anywhere introducing any of them. That is the gap this file exists to close, and it is why the dummy-friendly work is a metalanguage ramp rather than a prose lint.",
  "universality": "Universal by design. Every track needs the words for talking about its own language, and the ORDER is the pedagogy rather than the wording. A track whose language has no such category -- Spanish has no declension -- declares an omission rather than leaving a hole.",
